---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import PlanTheJob from "../components/PlanTheJob.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Portable Generators",
    "url": "/best-portable-generator/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Inverter Generators",
    "url": "/best-inverter-generator/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Dual Fuel Generators",
    "url": "/best-dual-fuel-generator/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Generators With a 50 Amp Outlet",
    "url": "/best-generator-with-50-amp-outlet/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best 10000 Watt Generators",
    "url": "/best-10000-watt-generator/"
  },
  {
    "title": "How to Store a Portable Generator",
    "url": "/how-to-store-a-portable-generator/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Generator Wattage Chart",
    "url": "/generator-wattage-chart/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-11-05", modified: "2026-11-05" });
const title = "6 Best Electric Start Generators in 2026: Push-Button & Remote";
const description =
  "The best electric start generators from 2,800W to 9,500W, with push-button, key and remote start compared, plus how to keep the battery alive for cold starts.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "westinghouse-igen5000",
    "name": "Westinghouse iGen5000",
    "category": "Inverter, 3,900W Running / 5,000W Peak, Remote Start",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Push-button electric start plus a wireless key fob, with a recoil cord as backup",
      "3,900 running and 5,000 peak watts covers a fridge, sump pump and lights",
      "Rated for up to 18 hours at 25% load on a 3.4-gallon tank",
      "Westinghouse lists 52 dBA, quiet enough to run near a house",
      "TT-30R RV outlet, a 5-20R duplex and two USB ports"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Westinghouse lists 104.7 lb dry, so it rolls rather than carries",
      "120V only, so it cannot feed a 240V well pump or a 240V transfer switch"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The easiest generator here to start from the back door: remote fob, inverter-quiet, and sized for the loads most households actually need in an outage.",
    "description": "The iGen5000 is the pick for most people who want electric start because it pairs the feature with a size you can live with. Westinghouse rates it at 3,900 running and 5,000 peak watts, which is enough for a refrigerator, a cycling sump pump, a furnace blower on a 120V circuit and lighting, and it lists up to 18 hours of runtime at quarter load on its 3.4-gallon tank. The start system is the full set: a push-button on the panel, a wireless key fob, and a recoil cord for the day the battery is flat. Because it is an inverter, it idles down at light load, and Westinghouse lists it at 52 dBA, so starting it from inside the house and leaving it running overnight is realistic. The limits are honest ones. It is 120V only, so a 240V well pump or a whole-panel transfer switch is out, and at 104.7 pounds dry it rolls on its wheels rather than being carried. If you want the same unit on propane too, Westinghouse also sells a dual fuel iGen5000DF. The iGen5000 replaces the older iGen4500 in Westinghouse's lineup, so expect to see both names in reviews."
  },
  {
    "id": "honda-eu7000is",
    "name": "Honda EU7000iS (EU7000ISNAND)",
    "category": "Inverter, 5,500W Running / 7,000W Surge, Fuel Injected",
    "badge": "Best for Cold Climates",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Electronic fuel injection on the GX390 engine means no choke to set in the cold",
      "12V button start with recoil backup",
      "120/240V with an L14-30R, so it can feed a 30 amp transfer switch",
      "Rated for roughly 16 to 18 hours at quarter load on a 5.1-gallon tank, depending on the listing",
      "Inverter output clean enough for electronics and furnace controls"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Around 261 lb, so it needs a flat path from storage to where it runs",
      "Expensive for 5,500 running watts compared with open-frame units"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "If you live where January mornings start below freezing, fuel injection matters more than any start button. This is the electric start generator that removes the choke from the equation.",
    "description": "Most cold-start failures are carburetor problems wearing a battery costume: the mixture is wrong, the choke is set badly, or varnish has narrowed a jet over the summer. The EU7000iS sidesteps that with electronic fuel injection on its 389cc GX390 engine, so there is no choke lever and the engine meters fuel for the temperature it is in. Pair that with the 12V button start and you have the simplest cold-morning routine in this list: turn on the fuel, press the button. Honda rates it at 5,500 running and 7,000 surge watts at 120/240V, and the panel carries two GFCI 5-20R outlets, an L5-30R and an L14-30R, so it can feed a 30 amp transfer switch or interlock inlet and run a 240V well pump. Honda's listings put runtime at roughly 16 to 18 hours at quarter load and about 6.5 hours at rated load from its 5.1-gallon tank. The trade-offs are weight and price. Listings put it around 261 pounds, and per watt it costs far more than the open-frame Westinghouse and Generac units here. You are paying for fuel injection, inverter-grade power and Honda's engine reputation, and for a cold climate that is money well spent."
  },
  {
    "id": "honda-eu3000is",
    "name": "Honda EU3000iS (EU3000IS1AN)",
    "category": "Inverter, 2,800W Running / 3,000W Max",
    "badge": "Best Small Electric Start",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "One of the few inverters near 3,000W with true electric start",
      "Listed for up to 19.6 hours at quarter load on 3.4 gallons",
      "Spec sheet lists about 50 dBA at quarter load and 57 dBA at rated load",
      "Recoil backup if the battery is drained",
      "Honda GX engine and dealer network for parts and service"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Roughly 131 lb for under 3,000 watts, heavy for its output",
      "120V only, and pricey per watt"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The small electric start generator to buy if you want quiet and reliability more than watts. It is heavy for its size, but it starts with a button and runs all night.",
    "description": "Electric start is rare below about 4,000 watts, because a battery and starter motor add weight and cost that buyers of small inverters usually do not want. The EU3000iS is the exception that has stayed in Honda's lineup for years. It is rated at 2,800 running watts and 3,000 maximum, enough for a refrigerator, a chest freezer, lights, a router and a CPAP at the same time, and Honda's listing claims up to 19.6 hours at quarter load from its 3.4-gallon tank. Spec sheets put noise at about 50 dB(A) at quarter load and 57 dB(A) at rated load, which makes it one of the quietest generators you can start with a button. The cost of that is weight: roughly 131 pounds for under 3,000 watts is heavy, and it sits on wheels rather than in your hand the way an EU2200i does. It is also 120V only. For an older owner who wants a small, quiet generator that never needs a pull, or for an RV with a 30 amp service and one air conditioner, it is the cleanest answer in its class."
  },
  {
    "id": "westinghouse-wgen7500dfc",
    "name": "Westinghouse WGen7500DFc",
    "category": "Dual Fuel, 7,500W Running / 9,500W Peak, Remote Start, CO Sensor",
    "badge": "Best Value Remote Start",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Push-button electric start with a wireless remote key fob",
      "7,500 running watts handles a well pump, fridge, freezer and furnace together",
      "L14-30R twist-lock for a 30 amp transfer switch or interlock inlet",
      "Dual fuel: 6,750 running watts on propane",
      "CO sensor with automatic shutdown"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Westinghouse lists 202.1 lb dry",
      "Open-frame engine noise, far louder than an inverter"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The cheapest way to get remote start on a generator big enough for real home backup. Loud, heavy, and very good at its job.",
    "description": "For whole-house essentials at 240V, the WGen7500DFc is where remote start stops being a premium feature. Westinghouse rates it at 7,500 running and 9,500 peak watts on gasoline and 6,750 running and 8,550 peak on propane, which covers a 240V well pump, a refrigerator, a freezer, a gas furnace blower and lighting on a typical outage plan, and it lists up to 11 hours of runtime from its 6.6-gallon tank. Starting is by a panel push-button or the included wireless key fob, with a recoil cord as backup, and the L14-30R twist-lock outlet is the one a 30 amp manual transfer switch or interlock inlet expects. The trade-offs are the open-frame ones. It is not an inverter, so it runs at full speed regardless of load, which costs fuel at light loads and makes it much louder than anything in the inverter class. Westinghouse lists 202.1 pounds dry. What tips it into this list over the plain gasoline WGen7500 is the c in the name: a CO sensor that shuts the engine down if exhaust builds up around it, which matters more on a remote start generator than on any other, because the whole point of a fob is starting a machine you are not looking at."
  },
  {
    "id": "generac-gp8000e-8929",
    "name": "Generac GP8000E COsense (Model 8929)",
    "category": "Open Frame, 8,000W Running / 10,000W Starting",
    "badge": "Best With CO Shutoff",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Electric start with the battery and a plug-in battery charger included",
      "COsense carbon monoxide shutdown built in",
      "8,000 running and 10,000 starting watts at 120/240V",
      "L14-30R plus GFCI 5-20R outlets",
      "50-state (CARB compliant) version of the GP8000E"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "No wireless remote start, only the panel switch",
      "Retail listings put it around 207 lb"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The pick for buyers who care more about a CO shutoff than a key fob. Push-button start, real 240V capacity, and a safety system that turns it off if the exhaust comes back toward you.",
    "description": "The GP8000E gives up remote start but adds something more important for many households: COsense, Generac's carbon monoxide shutdown, which stops the engine if CO builds up around the unit. Generac rates it at 8,000 running and 10,000 starting watts at 120/240V, and its operator manual lists 66.6 amps at 120V and 33.3 amps at 240V, so the L14-30R twist-lock can deliver the full 30 amps a typical manual transfer switch is built for. Starting is electric, and Generac ships the battery with a plug-in charger and puts a 12V battery charger input on the panel, which is exactly the storage habit this guide recommends; there is a recoil cord if the battery is dead anyway. Model 8929 is the 50-state, CARB-compliant version. The 420cc engine uses Generac's PowerRush system, which the company rates for more starting capacity than a conventional design of the same size, useful when a well pump and an air conditioner compressor kick on together. The cons are practical. There is no key fob, so someone still walks out to the generator to start it, and retail listings put the weight around 207 pounds. For a family that will run it during storms at night, the CO shutoff is the better safety feature to own."
  },
  {
    "id": "westinghouse-wgen9500df",
    "name": "Westinghouse WGen9500DF",
    "category": "Dual Fuel, 9,500W Running / 12,500W Peak, Remote Start",
    "badge": "Most Power",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Push-button, wireless remote fob and recoil start, battery included",
      "9,500 running watts on gasoline, 8,500 on propane",
      "Both an L14-30R and a 14-50R outlet",
      "Westinghouse lists up to 12 hours at 25% load on 6.6 gallons",
      "Propane option sidesteps stale gasoline in a stored unit"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Westinghouse lists 211 lb dry",
      "74 dBA rating, loud next to an inverter"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The electric start generator to buy when the plan includes central-air-sized loads or a 50 amp RV. Remote start and dual fuel on one panel.",
    "description": "At the top of the range, the WGen9500DF combines every start method with enough output for a heavy outage plan. Westinghouse rates it at 9,500 running and 12,500 peak watts on gasoline and 8,500 running and 11,200 peak on propane, and its panel includes two GFCI 5-20R outlets, an L14-30R twist-lock and a 14-50R, which means it can feed a 30 amp transfer switch or a 50 amp RV or inlet. Start it with the panel push-button, the included remote key fob or the recoil cord; the battery ships with it. Westinghouse lists up to 12 hours at quarter load on its 6.6-gallon tank. Dual fuel is the quiet advantage for electric start owners: a generator that sits for months on stale gasoline is the most common reason a good battery cranks an engine that will not fire, and propane does not go stale. The costs are size and noise. Westinghouse lists 211 pounds dry and a 74 dBA rating, so it belongs on a pad away from bedroom windows. If you mainly need the 50 amp outlet, our guide to the best generators with a 50 amp outlet covers that side in detail."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Electric Start Generators",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-electric-start-generator/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is an electric start generator worth it?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most generators above about 4,000 watts, yes. The engines are large enough that pulling a cord is real effort, and push-button start lets anyone in the household start it. Below 3,500 watts it matters less, because small inverters start easily by hand and electric start adds weight, cost and a battery to maintain."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does a generator starting battery last?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Expect a few years with good care, less if it is stored discharged or in extreme heat or cold. The battery is usually a small 12V sealed lead-acid unit. Keeping it on a maintainer during storage and exercising the generator monthly extends its life considerably, and replacement batteries are inexpensive."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does a generator charge its own battery?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most electric start generators have a charging circuit that charges the battery while the engine runs. That keeps a healthy battery topped up, but a short monthly run may not recover a deeply drained one. For storage longer than a few weeks, a battery maintainer on the battery or the charging port is the reliable answer."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I start an electric start generator with a dead battery?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, on every model in this guide, by using the recoil pull cord. Many can also be jump-started from a 12V jump pack or spare battery connected at the battery terminals; follow the manual's procedure and observe polarity. If you cannot pull a cord, keep a charged jump pack with the generator."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the difference between electric start and remote start?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Electric start uses a battery and starter motor triggered by a button or key on the generator. Remote start adds a wireless fob, or occasionally an app, that triggers the same starter from a distance. Every remote start generator also has electric start; not every electric start generator has remote start."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will an electric start generator start in cold weather?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually, if the battery is charged, the choke is set correctly and the oil is the right grade. Cold reduces battery output and thickens oil, so a weak battery is most likely to fail on a cold morning. Fuel-injected models like the Honda EU7000iS remove the choke step, which makes cold starts the most dependable."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do push button start generators need maintenance?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "They need everything a recoil generator needs, such as oil changes, fresh fuel and spark plugs, plus battery care. Check the battery terminals for corrosion, keep the battery charged during storage, and test the start every month. Replace the fob coin cell on remote models when the range drops."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Home Emergency Preparedness",
    tags: [
      "electric start generator",
      "electric start portable generator",
      "generator electric start",
      "push button start generator",
      "remote start generator",
      "best electric start generator"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated November 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          6 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Electric Start Generators</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          A push button or a key fob means anyone in the house can start the generator, in any weather, without yanking a cord. The catch is a small battery that fails if you ignore it. These are the electric start models worth buying, and how to make sure they start when you need them.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#westinghouse-igen5000"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            See All 6 Picks
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="6.4k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. The 6 Best Picks</a>
          <a href="#who-needs-it" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Who Actually Needs an Electric Start Generator</a>
          <a href="#start-types" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Push-Button vs Key vs Remote vs App Start</a>
          <a href="#battery" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. The Battery Is the Weak Point</a>
          <a href="#trickle-charging" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Trickle Charging and Storage</a>
          <a href="#cold-starts" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Cold Starts</a>
          <a href="#recoil-fallback" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. The Recoil Backup</a>
          <a href="#sizing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Sizing an Electric Start Generator</a>
          <a href="#remote-safety" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Remote Start and Safety</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. FAQ</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. Common Mistakes</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Electric start is the feature that decides whether the generator gets used. A 400cc engine on a recoil cord is a real physical effort, and in an outage it often falls to whoever is home, not whoever bought it. The six generators below start with a button, a key fob or both, and every one keeps a pull cord as backup. The part most buyers overlook is the battery: it is the weak point of every electric start generator, and the sections after the picks explain how to keep it from failing on the coldest morning of the year.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />


        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The 6 Best Electric Start Generators, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ranked on start convenience, cold-start reliability, usable output and value. Specs come from manufacturer pages and spec sheets; where listings disagree we say so or leave the number out. Every pick has push-button or key start and a recoil backup, and three add a wireless remote fob.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <div id={product.id} class="scroll-mt-24 mt-12 first:mt-8">
              <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-sm hover:shadow-md transition-shadow">
                <div class="bg-gray-900 px-6 py-4 flex items-center justify-between">
                  <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
                    <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black">
                      {index + 1}
                    </span>
                    {productImages[product.id] && (
                      <img
                        src={productImages[product.id]}
                        alt={product.name}
                        loading="lazy"
                        width="40"
                        height="40"
                        class="w-10 h-10 rounded-lg bg-white object-contain p-1 shrink-0"
                      />
                    )}
                    <div>
                      <h3 class="text-white font-black text-lg leading-snug m-0"><span class="block text-[#38BDF8] text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wide mb-1 sm:hidden">{product.badge}</span>{product.name}</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs">{product.category}</p>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                  <span class="bg-[#38BDF8]/20 text-[#38BDF8] px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold hidden sm:block">
                    {product.badge}
                  </span>
                </div>
                <div class="p-6">
                  {product.rating && product.reviewCount && (
